WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.620
Today, I want to talk about thinking
in the language we are learning.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:05.840
It comes up all the time.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.690
How do I get to where I can think
in the language I'm learning?

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:14.109
To me, this is related to this other
question of how can I speak like a native?

00:00:14.189 --> 00:00:18.469
And here, I think we need to understand,
at least my view of the whole process

00:00:18.469 --> 00:00:22.899
of language learning is we start out
at basically zero, knowing nothing

00:00:22.899 --> 00:00:26.269
or very little, we may know related
languages, but we're at the very

00:00:26.269 --> 00:00:28.659
beginning and there's this continuous.

00:00:29.014 --> 00:00:34.765
Sort of voyage, which eventually takes
us to a higher level, but that higher

00:00:34.765 --> 00:00:38.615
level is very rarely, if ever, perfection.

00:00:38.715 --> 00:00:42.564
I just don't think perfection
is a term we can think of when

00:00:42.565 --> 00:00:43.605
we think of language learning.

00:00:44.294 --> 00:00:48.520
If we come to this question of thinking
in the language So even languages that

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:54.020
I speak very well in, say, my own case,
in Japanese, in French, there are always

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.770
times when I will translate from English.

00:00:56.899 --> 00:00:58.680
I may not do that for the longest time.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.169
I might feel very comfortable in the
language, but there will inevitably

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:06.920
come some point where I am actually
using a structure or a word that I've

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:08.820
reached into my brain for in English.

00:01:09.015 --> 00:01:10.845
And I translate it into the language.

00:01:10.945 --> 00:01:12.055
It's a continuum.

00:01:12.195 --> 00:01:15.695
So, as we're getting better and better
in the language, there are moments when

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:17.374
we hear something, it's instant meaning.

00:01:17.715 --> 00:01:19.424
We read something, it's instant meaning.

00:01:19.735 --> 00:01:21.785
We go to say something
and it just comes out.

00:01:22.085 --> 00:01:26.034
And so there, we are, in a way,
thinking in the language we're

00:01:26.034 --> 00:01:30.375
learning, but we're doing it for, you
know, a small part of the time that

00:01:30.375 --> 00:01:31.515
we are spending with the language.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.539
As we continue, as we become more
comfortable, we get better at it.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:39.780
As with so much in language learning,
the deliberate effort to learn

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.550
something is often counterproductive.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.100
The deliberate effort to avoid
thinking in the language just makes

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:50.219
you uptight and makes it, you know,
less likely that you will achieve that.

00:01:50.379 --> 00:01:52.529
You have to relax and let it happen.

00:01:52.559 --> 00:01:54.029
It will gradually happen.

00:01:54.229 --> 00:01:58.270
More and more, you will start thinking
in the language, if you continue with

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.259
all of your language learning activities.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:04.220
And I think this concept of Thinking
in the language is related to

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:05.999
sort of speaking like a native.

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:09.920
I have said before, I don't think
we can achieve a hundred percent,

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:11.360
you know, sounding like a native.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.469
There will always be something
that betrays you as not a

00:02:14.469 --> 00:02:17.610
speaker of that language, but
you can become very, very good.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:21.630
And in this whole issue of this sort
of continuum, where we start in the

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:23.070
language and you get better and better.

00:02:23.435 --> 00:02:26.954
It brings up these two terms that
I see very often from linguists.

00:02:26.984 --> 00:02:30.635
One is interlanguage and
the other is fossilization.

00:02:30.965 --> 00:02:35.365
And apparently those two terms
were developed by a professor

00:02:35.395 --> 00:02:38.015
of linguistics, Larry Selnicker.

00:02:38.105 --> 00:02:40.974
So interlanguage explains the ability.

00:02:41.265 --> 00:02:45.525
The ability of people to speak better in
the language they're learning because of

00:02:45.525 --> 00:02:47.375
the influence of their native language.

00:02:47.575 --> 00:02:51.565
And so structures and even vocabulary
items, not to mention pronunciation

00:02:51.785 --> 00:02:55.945
from their native language will
influence how they speak in

00:02:55.954 --> 00:02:57.475
the language they're learning.

00:02:57.635 --> 00:02:58.765
To me, that's obvious.

00:02:58.765 --> 00:03:01.734
I'm not sure why we need
a term like interlanguage.

00:03:02.045 --> 00:03:05.565
Native speakers of certain languages
will tend to make the same kinds of

00:03:05.565 --> 00:03:09.884
mistakes that reflect patterns and
structures in their own language.

00:03:10.109 --> 00:03:11.250
They don't all do that.

00:03:11.459 --> 00:03:12.549
Some do, some don't.

00:03:12.769 --> 00:03:17.015
But to some extent, these
patterns are Reflective of the

00:03:17.015 --> 00:03:18.185
native language of the speaker.

00:03:18.225 --> 00:03:19.175
That's normal.

00:03:19.235 --> 00:03:23.155
We use what we know and what we're
comfortable using, and we apply that

00:03:23.165 --> 00:03:24.345
to the things that we're learning.

00:03:24.445 --> 00:03:29.295
It's even suggested that learners
might speak or produce the language

00:03:29.305 --> 00:03:31.445
better in a classroom drill setting.

00:03:31.695 --> 00:03:35.765
But then in a casual conversation,
they revert back to interlanguage.

00:03:35.805 --> 00:03:38.485
But that to me is normal because
you can have a structured

00:03:38.495 --> 00:03:39.735
environment in a classroom.

00:03:39.905 --> 00:03:41.575
You can have the impression
that something's been

00:03:41.575 --> 00:03:43.505
taught, but once you revert.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:47.700
Say, a couple of days later, when you're
in a normal situation, and possibly a

00:03:47.859 --> 00:03:52.630
stressful situation, then that deliberate
learning may not help you that much,

00:03:52.699 --> 00:03:56.769
although probably does contribute to
gradually getting better in the language.

00:03:57.869 --> 00:04:02.760
Again, fossilization is presented as some
kind of a final stage in interlanguage

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:07.029
because some learners simply don't
progress beyond a certain point.

00:04:07.029 --> 00:04:11.619
So they're stuck at this interlanguage
or this less than optimal level in the

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.450
language and they don't get better.

00:04:13.640 --> 00:04:16.029
But I think we continue to
think in our own language to

00:04:16.029 --> 00:04:17.870
some extent, varying degrees.

00:04:17.899 --> 00:04:20.760
We continue to use structures
that reflect our native language.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:21.829
That's always the case.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:25.019
But I think if we put enough effort
into listening and reading, and if we

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:28.879
are relaxed about it, and don't say,
I want to speak better, but we aren't

00:04:28.879 --> 00:04:33.429
prepared to put in the hours of listening
and hours of listening and Thousands

00:04:33.429 --> 00:04:37.450
and hundreds of thousands of words of
reading to allow our brains to get a

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:40.609
better sense of the language, then we're
not going to get beyond that point.

00:04:40.669 --> 00:04:44.789
However, it's important to remember
that many speakers of a second

00:04:44.789 --> 00:04:47.070
language are, in effect, fossilized.

00:04:47.119 --> 00:04:51.520
They have come to a level, it might be
a very high level, where they are happy.

00:04:52.275 --> 00:04:54.865
And I'm this way in a number
of languages that I speak.

00:04:54.885 --> 00:04:55.625
Good enough.

00:04:55.695 --> 00:04:56.854
I can communicate.

00:04:56.905 --> 00:04:57.905
I understand.

00:04:58.205 --> 00:04:59.604
People understand me.

00:04:59.675 --> 00:05:00.255
I'm good.

00:05:00.354 --> 00:05:00.854
I'm happy.

00:05:00.864 --> 00:05:04.925
It's even suggested in one of these papers
that, to some extent, the interlanguage

00:05:04.944 --> 00:05:06.705
may be an expression of identity.

00:05:06.844 --> 00:05:08.195
I think that's unlikely.

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:12.800
However, I've mentioned this before, even
my father, who spoke English very, very

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:15.460
well, had larger vocabulary than I did.

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:19.630
I think at some level it bothered him
that English spelling was so inconsistent

00:05:20.009 --> 00:05:24.659
and therefore he would pronounce English
words as if they were written in Czech.

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:29.950
So as I've said, Bank of Nova Scotia
was Scotia, Nova Scotia, because

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:32.750
to him somehow TIA should be.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:37.729
And so there's a sort of a
psychological element of resisting

00:05:37.909 --> 00:05:41.669
the new language, which is also, I
think, not an unimportant factor.

00:05:41.979 --> 00:05:44.189
But my father thought in the
language, I'm a hundred percent

00:05:44.189 --> 00:05:47.369
sure he thought in English, but
he didn't speak like a native.

00:05:47.559 --> 00:05:50.719
So it's possible to get to a level
where we think in the language

00:05:50.729 --> 00:05:52.119
where it's instant meaning.

00:05:52.390 --> 00:05:57.074
We express ourselves, not necessarily like
a native, but without thinking about it.

00:05:57.455 --> 00:06:00.705
So we are thinking that we're in
the language, but our utterances are

00:06:00.705 --> 00:06:02.174
not a hundred percent native like.

00:06:02.234 --> 00:06:06.944
All of these things, to my mind, are part
of language learning, of communicating,

00:06:06.994 --> 00:06:11.445
of the fact that we cannot expect
perfection in language learning, and

00:06:11.445 --> 00:06:15.454
we shouldn't beat ourselves up if we
don't yet feel that we think in the

00:06:15.465 --> 00:06:19.044
language as well as we would like, or
we aren't as fluent as we would like.

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:21.150
All of these things are perfectly normal.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:26.210
And so I would leave you with a couple of
videos I did on the whole issue of errors.

00:06:26.229 --> 00:06:30.390
The importance of errors, if we recognize
our own errors, that helps us learn.

00:06:30.679 --> 00:06:33.679
But at the same time, learning
to live with our less than

00:06:33.689 --> 00:06:35.440
perfect use of the language.

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:36.429
Bye for now.

